=== Natural generation ===
Birch leaves occur naturally on [[birch]] trees throughout the [[Overworld]]. They can also generate as part of [[abandoned camp]]s in [[birch forest]]s, [[flower forest]]s and [[forest]]s.

{{block distribution}}

=== Post-generation ===
{{see also|Tutorial:Tree farming}}
Birch leaves generate as part of birch trees grown from [[birch sapling]]s.

== Usage ==
{{Main|Leaves#Usage}}
Birch leaves that decay have the same drop chances as when broken by players, yielding [[birch sapling]]s 5% ({{frac|1|20}}) of the time, [[stick]]s 2% of the time, and otherwise nothing. Leaves that are burned do not yield saplings.

=== Smelting ingredient ===
{{Smelting
|showname=1
|Birch Leaves
|Leaf Litter
|0.1}}

=== Redstone component ===
{{Exclusive|java|section=1}}
The state of a leaves block—including a player-placed block—changes after 1 game tick (half a redstone tick) when the distance to the nearest [[log]] or [[wood]] block changes, up to 6 blocks of leaves away. [[Observer]]s facing away from the leaves detect this change and transmit a redstone signal in the same game tick, making leaves useful for redstone signal transmission.

=== Piston interactivity ===
Leaves are destroyed when pushed by [[piston]]s. They do not stick to [[sticky piston]]s, [[slime block]]s, or [[honey block]]s.

=== Composting ===
Birch leaves have a 30% chance of increasing the compost level in a [[composter]] by 1. A stack of leaves yields an average of 3.05 [[bone meal]].

=== Color ===
[[File:Birch (colormap).png|<samp>birch.png</samp> colormap|thumb|150px]]
Unlike most other leaves, the color of birch leaves does not change with the [[biome]] they are placed in, but always stays green. The texture is always colored with {{color|#80a755}}.

{{IN|be}}, this color is picked from a [[Biome#Tint|colormap]] which allows biome tinting, though the color is the same in every biome.

==== Snowy leaves ====
{{Exclusive|Bedrock|section=1}}
[[File:Snowing BE.jpg|thumb|Trees in a snowy biome during snowfall in ''Bedrock Edition'']]

In any biome with a base [[Biome#Temperature|temperature]] of below 0.15, birch leaves gradually fade to white once [[snowfall]] begins, giving them a frosted appearance for the storm's duration. During transition, the white color is blended with the regular color in eight steps. After snowfall ends, leaves gradually fade back to their normal coloration.

This does not affect leaves in biomes with a higher base temperature, even at altitudes that allow snowfall. The change is entirely invisible to the client if the graphics mode is set to Simple, though it still takes place, and leaves are the correct color phase for the time if the option is re-enabled.

=== Particles ===
Birch leaves produce falling leaves [[particles]] underneath the leaf block, where they slowly fall to the ground. Their color corresponds to the leaves block they fall from, which can change during snowfall.{{only|be|short=1}}

